1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,480
Morning breath, if brushing before bed never even crossed your mind, pay attention — that small habit matters more than most people think.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:14,640
Saliva production drops sharply while you sleep, and that's what lets odor-causing bacteria take over.

3
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:20,880
Anaerobic bacteria on the back of your tongue break down proteins into sulfur compounds overnight.

4
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,760
That's the smell you notice the moment you wake up.

5
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:36,880
Brushing and scraping your tongue right before bed, not just in the morning, can cut how strong morning breath feels by roughly half — it removes the bacteria's food source before hours of low saliva let them multiply.

6
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,880
Mouth breathing makes it worse.

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:44,560
Sleeping with your mouth open, from congestion or snoring, dries out your mouth all night.

8
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:55,600
A saline rinse before bed and bedroom humidity between 40 and 60 percent help you breathe through your nose instead; if congestion keeps coming back, get it checked out.

9
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:03,200
Dehydration adds to it too — not enough water, or alcohol before bed, means even less saliva to begin with.

10
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:15,600
Spread your water intake through the evening instead of all at once, and try a cup of green tea earlier in the day: its polyphenols neutralize sulfur compounds and curb the bacteria that make them.
